[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"React Components: Functional vs. Class Components"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React components are the building blocks of any React application. They allow you to divide the UI into independent, reusable pieces. Historically, React offered two primary ways to define components: functional components and class components. While functional components are now the recommended approach, understanding both types is crucial for working with existing codebases and grasping React's evolution."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Class Components"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Class components are ES6 classes that extend `React.Component`. They have a `render()` method that returns the JSX to be rendered. Class components can also manage their own state and have access to lifecycle methods."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

class Welcome extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { message: 'Hello, world!' };
  }

  render() {
    return <h1>{this.state.message}</h1>;
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `Welcome` is a class component. It initializes its state in the constructor and renders a heading with the message from the state.  The `props` are passed to the constructor via `super(props)`."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"State Management in Class Components"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Class components manage their state using `this.state` and update it using `this.setState()`.  `setState()` triggers a re-render of the component."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

class Counter extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { count: 0 };
  }

  increment = () => {
    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>
        <button onClick={this.increment}>Increment</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `Counter` component maintains a `count` in its state. The `increment` method updates the state, causing the component to re-render and display the updated count."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Methods in Class Components"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Class components have lifecycle methods that allow you to perform actions at specific points in the component's lifecycle, such as when it's mounted, updated, or unmounted. Common lifecycle methods include `componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate`, and `componentWillUnmount`."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

class Example extends React.Component {
  componentDidMount() {
    console.log('Component mounted!');
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) {
    console.log('Component updated!');
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    console.log('Component will unmount!');
  }

  render() {
    return <div>Example Component</div>;
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Functional Components"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functional components are simpler than class components. They are JavaScript functions that accept props as an argument and return JSX. Before React 16.8 (when Hooks were introduced), functional components were primarily used for presentational purposes, as they couldn't manage their own state or access lifecycle methods. Now, with Hooks, functional components can do almost everything class components can do, and are generally preferred for their simplicity and readability."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

function Welcome(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is a simple functional component that receives `props` and renders a greeting.  It's more concise than the class component equivalent."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"State Management with Hooks (useState)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useState` Hook allows functional components to manage state. It returns a state variable and a function to update it."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `useState(0)` initializes the `count` state variable to 0. `setCount` is the function used to update the `count`.  Clicking the button calls `setCount`, which triggers a re-render."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Side Effects with Hooks (useEffect)"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useEffect` Hook allows functional components to perform side effects, such as data fetching, subscriptions, or manually changing the DOM. It's similar to `componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate`, and `componentWillUnmount` combined."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function DataFetcher() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchData() {
      const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1');
      const json = await response.json();
      setData(json);
    }

    fetchData();
  }, []); // The empty array means this effect runs only once, on mount

  if (!data) {
    return <p>Loading...</p>;
  }

  return <p>Data: {data.title}</p>;
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This component fetches data from an API when it mounts. The empty dependency array `[]` passed as the second argument to `useEffect` ensures that the effect only runs once, similar to `componentDidMount`. If the dependency array contained a variable, the effect would run again whenever that variable changed."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Functional Components with Memoization (React.memo)"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"To optimize functional components and prevent unnecessary re-renders, you can use `React.memo`. It's a higher-order component that memoizes the component, re-rendering it only when its props have changed."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

const MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent(props) {
  // Only re-renders if props change
  return <p>Value: {props.value}</p>;
});

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing Between Functional and Class Components"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Functional Components with Hooks (Recommended):** For new projects, functional components with Hooks are generally preferred due to their simplicity, readability, and performance benefits. They encourage writing smaller, more focused components."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Class Components:** Still relevant for maintaining older codebases or when you need features not easily achievable with Hooks (though this is rare).  Understanding class components is essential for working on legacy React projects."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Summarized"},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Syntax:** Functional components are functions, while class components are ES6 classes."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**State Management:** Class components use `this.state` and `this.setState()`. Functional components use the `useState` Hook."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Lifecycle Methods:** Class components have lifecycle methods like `componentDidMount`. Functional components use the `useEffect` Hook to handle side effects."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Readability and Conciseness:** Functional components with Hooks are often more concise and easier to read."},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"In conclusion, while class components have their place in React's history and are still encountered in older projects, functional components with Hooks are the modern and recommended approach for building React applications. They offer a more streamlined and efficient way to manage state, handle side effects, and create reusable UI components."}]
